\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Integral Pfaffian identities}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Let #{b} be an alternating form on the finite free
module #{E} of rank #{2s}, and let #{\omega} be a volume
form as in the \vocabk{ring Pfaffian}{fdip-00UR}.
For every endomorphism #{P:E\to E}, its pullback
#{(P^*b)(x,y)=b(Px,Py)} satisfies
##{\pfaffian_\omega(P^*b)=\det(P)\pfaffian_\omega(b).}
Define #{b^\flat:E\to E^*} by #{b^\flat(x)=b(x,-)},
and let #{\xi\in\Lambda^{2s}E} satisfy #{\omega(\xi)=1}.
Then
##{(\Lambda^{2s}b^\flat)(\xi)
       =\pfaffian_\omega(b)^2\omega.}
Neither identity requires #{P} or #{b^\flat} invertible.}

\proof{
\p{First use an ordered basis of #{E} with dual basis
#{z_1,\ldots,z_{2s}} and volume
#{\omega_0=z_1\cdots z_{2s}}. Let #{A} represent #{b}
and write #{\pfaffian(A)=\pfaffian_{\omega_0}(b)}.
Then #{a_b=\sum_{i<j}A_{ij}z_iz_j}. In the finite
product defining #{\extexp(ta_b)}, a
nonzero term of degree #{t^s} selects disjoint pairs covering
all generators. Even pair words commute, and sorting the
selected word gives precisely its matching sign. This proves
the matching formula
##{\pfaffian(A)=\sum_{\mathfrak m}\varepsilon(\mathfrak m)
                      \prod_{(i,j)\in\mathfrak m}A_{ij}.}
Here each pair in a perfect matching has #{i<j}, pairs
are ordered by their first index, and
#{\varepsilon(\mathfrak m)} is the sign of the resulting
word of #{2s} indices. Substitute
#{z_i\mapsto\sum_jP_{ij}z_j}. The alternating quadratic
coefficient matrix becomes #{P^{\mathsf T}AP}, including
zero diagonal. Naturality in \ref{fdip-00TH} carries the
#{s}-th divided coefficient to the new one. The top word
is multiplied by #{\det P}, by its finite alternating
expansion. Comparing coefficients proves congruence over
#{k} itself:
#{\pfaffian(P^{\mathsf T}AP)=\det(P)\pfaffian(A)}.}

\p{For the square identity, take the generic alternating
matrix with independent strict-upper entries over
#{k_0=\mathbb Z[a_{ij}:i<j]}. The claimed equality is between
two polynomials in this domain. Work temporarily in its
fraction field and induct on #{s}; the empty identity is
#{1=1}. When #{s>0}, the pivot #{a=a_{12}} is nonzero.
For each #{j>2}, replace the basis vector by
##{e'_j=e_j+\frac{a_{2j}}a e_1-\frac{a_{1j}}a e_2,\qquad
 e'_1=e_1,\quad e'_2=e_2.}
The change has determinant one. Direct evaluation gives
#{A(e'_1,e'_j)=A(e'_2,e'_j)=0}, so the new matrix is
##{\begin{pmatrix}0&a\\-a&0\end{pmatrix}\oplus A_0.}
In its matching sum, the first index must pair with the
second; hence its Pfaffian is #{a\pfaffian(A_0)}.
Its determinant is #{a^2\det A_0}. Congruence and the
inductive identity in rank #{2s-2} prove
#{\det A=\pfaffian(A)^2} in the fraction field.}

\p{The domain #{k_0} injects into its fraction field, so the
two integer polynomials were already equal in #{k_0}.
Specializing their variables proves the identity over every
commutative ring. The pivot denominator belongs only to the
proof field; no denominator is inverted in the target ring.}

\p{The matrix of #{b^\flat} is #{A^{\mathsf T}}, whose
determinant is #{\det A}; thus the square identity is the
claimed identity between top exterior powers for #{\omega_0}.
For an arbitrary volume #{\omega=c\omega_0}, the scalar
#{c} is a unit, #{\xi=c^{-1}\xi_0}, and
#{\pfaffian_\omega(b)=c^{-1}\pfaffian_{\omega_0}(b)}.
Both sides of the exterior-power identity scale by
#{c^{-1}}; both sides of the pullback identity scale by
#{c^{-1}} as well. This proves the statements for the
chosen volume. In rank zero the prescribed volume is
#{1}, and both statements reduce to #{1=1}.}
}
